    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s3-n22",
        "text": "[ZH] 交流电路中的有效电阻\n交流电的电压和电流会随时间变化。\n本课先研究最简单的情形：交流电源向一个理想的欧姆电阻供电。\n\n图 34 表示导线和电阻负载接到交流电源的两端。\n我们把电阻 $R$ 看作保持不变，并忽略导线的电阻。\n原书假定负载的自感系数很小、电阻较大。\n\n本课的**纯电阻**模型忽略电感和电容的影响。\n这个近似必须在所研究的交流频率下成立，也就是电路的电抗相对电阻可以忽略。\n这里的“纯”说的是理想模型中只有电阻 $R$ 起作用，并不是因为它消耗能量才没有电抗。\n\n电阻把电源提供的电能转化为内能。\n理想的电感、电容则能暂时储存能量并把能量送回电路。\n\n原书把这种电阻称为“有效电阻”。\n本课采用“电阻”或“纯电阻”，不要把它与电压、电流的**有效值（均方根值，RMS）**混淆：有效值是描述变化的电压、电流大小的一种方法，不是另一种电阻。\n\n图 34\n\n设电阻两端的电压按余弦规律变化：\n$u=U_{\\mathrm{m}}\\cos\\omega t$。\n这里，t 表示时间，ω 表示角频率。\n\n小写字母表示某一时刻的瞬时值，大写字母带原书的振幅下标表示最大大小。\n这个下标不是有效值的标记。\n\n在上述理想欧姆电阻模型中，任一时刻的电压和电流都满足欧姆定律。\n因此可以像第 21 节讨论的那样，用同一时刻的电压除以电阻，求出电流的瞬时值：\n\n$i=\\frac{u}{R}=\\frac{U_{\\mathrm{m}}\\cos\\omega t}{R}=I_{\\mathrm{m}}\\cos\\omega t$ （2.14）\n\n图 35\n\n**在理想纯电阻上，电流和电压同相**（图 35）。\n也就是说，它们同时到达正的最大值，同时过零，也同时到达负的最小值。\n\n图中电压、电流共用时间轴，但纵向没有数值刻度，两条曲线的高度不能用来比较伏特和安培的数值。\n电流的振幅由电压振幅与电阻决定：\n\n$I_{\\mathrm{m}}=\\frac{U_{\\mathrm{m}}}{R}$ （2.15）"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s3-n22",
        "text": "[EN] Pure resistance in an AC circuit\nAn alternating voltage and the current it produces change with time.\nWe will start with the simplest case: an AC source supplying an ideal ohmic resistor.\n\nFigure 34 shows wires and a resistive load connected to the two terminals of an AC source.\nWe treat its resistance $R$ as constant and neglect the resistance of the wires.\n\nThe source textbook assumes a small self-inductance and a relatively large resistance.\nIn this model, we neglect the effects of inductance and capacitance.\n\nThe approximation must hold at the frequency being studied: the reactive part of the circuit is negligible compared with its resistance.\nThe word \"pure\" describes a model in which only resistance $R$ matters.\n\nEnergy dissipation does not itself make reactance disappear.\nA resistor converts electrical energy supplied by the source into internal energy, while ideal inductors and capacitors can temporarily store energy and return it to the circuit.\n\nThe source textbook calls this \"effective resistance.\"\nWe use \"resistance\" or \"pure resistance\" here: do not confuse this with the **effective, or root-mean-square (RMS), value** of a voltage or current, which describes the size of a changing voltage or current, not a different kind of resistance.\n\nFigure 34\n\nSuppose the voltage across the resistor varies as a cosine:\n$u=U_{\\mathrm{m}}\\cos\\omega t$.\nHere, t is time and ω is angular frequency.\n\nLowercase letters represent instantaneous values: values at a particular moment.\nA capital letter with the source textbook's amplitude subscript represents the maximum magnitude, not an RMS value.\n\nFor the ideal ohmic resistor described above, Ohm's law relates voltage and current at every instant.\nAs in Section 21, dividing the voltage at that instant by the resistance gives the instantaneous current:\n\n$i=\\frac{u}{R}=\\frac{U_{\\mathrm{m}}\\cos\\omega t}{R}=I_{\\mathrm{m}}\\cos\\omega t$ (2.14)\n\nFigure 35\n\n**Current and voltage are in phase in an ideal pure resistance** (Figure 35).\nThey reach their positive maxima together, cross zero together, and reach their negative minima together.\n\nThe curves share a time axis, but there is no numerical vertical scale: their drawn heights do not compare numerical values in volts and amperes.\nThe current amplitude is determined by the voltage amplitude and the resistance:\n\n$I_{\\mathrm{m}}=\\frac{U_{\\mathrm{m}}}{R}$ (2.15)"
      }
